00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:45,230
Fight the future.

2
00:09:18,461 --> 00:09:24,461
The X-Files is a mystery, science fiction, thriller film directed by Rob Bowman.

3
00:13:57,692 --> 00:14:03,692
Mulder and Scully, now taken off the FBI's X Files cases, must find a way to fight the shadowy elements of the
government to find out the truth about a conspiracy that might mean the alien colonization of Earth.

4
00:18:36,923 --> 00:18:42,923
Agent Fox Mulder, played by David Duchovny.

5
00:23:16,153 --> 00:23:22,153
Agent Dana Scully, played by Gillian Anderson.

6
00:27:55,384 --> 00:28:01,384
Assistant Director Walter Skinner, played by Mitch Pileggi.

7
00:32:34,615 --> 00:32:40,615
The Cigarette-Smoking Man, played by William B. Davis.

8
00:37:13,846 --> 00:37:19,846
The Well-Manicured Man, played by John Neville.

9
00:41:53,076 --> 00:41:59,076
Kurtzweil, played by Martin Landau.

10
00:46:32,307 --> 00:46:38,307
The X-Files (also known as The X-Files: Fight the Future) is a 1998 American science fiction thriller film based on Chris Carter's television
series of the same name, which revolves around fictional unsolved cases called the X-Files and the characters solving them.

11
00:51:11,538 --> 00:51:17,538
It was directed by Rob Bowman, written by Carter and Frank Spotnitz and featured five main characters from the
television series: David Duchovny, Gillian Anderson, Mitch Pileggi, John Neville, and William B.

12
00:55:50,769 --> 00:55:56,769
Davis reprise their respective roles as FBI agents Fox Mulder and Dana Scully, FBI Assistant
Director Walter Skinner, Well-Manicured Man, and the Cigarette-Smoking Man.

13
01:00:30,000 --> 01:00:36,000
The film was promoted with the tagline Fight the Future.

14
01:05:09,230 --> 01:05:15,230
The film takes place between seasons five (episode "The End") and six (episode "The Beginning") of
the television series, and is based upon the series' extraterrestrial mythology.

15
01:09:48,461 --> 01:09:54,461
The story follows agents Mulder and Scully, removed from their usual jobs on the X-Files, and
investigating the bombing of a building and the destruction of criminal evidence.

16
01:14:27,692 --> 01:14:33,692
They uncover what appears to be a government conspiracy attempting to
hide the truth about an alien colonization of Earth.

17
01:19:06,923 --> 01:19:12,923
Carter decided to make a feature film to explore the show's
mythology on a wider scale and appeal to non-fans.

18
01:23:46,153 --> 01:23:52,153
He wrote the story with Frank Spotnitz at the end of 1996 and, with a budget from 20th
Century Fox, filming began in 1997, following the end of the show's fourth season.

19
01:28:25,384 --> 01:28:31,384
Carter assembled cast and crew from the show, as well as some other, well-known actors such as Blythe
Danner and Martin Landau, to begin production on what they termed "Project Blackwood".

20
01:33:04,615 --> 01:33:10,615
The film was produced by Carter and Daniel Sackheim.

21
01:37:43,846 --> 01:37:49,846
Mark Snow continued his role as X-Files composer to create the film's score.

22
01:42:23,076 --> 01:42:29,076
The film premiered on June 19, 1998, in the United States, and received mixed reviews from critics, but
was a box office success, earning $189 million worldwide against a budget of $66 million.

23
01:47:02,307 --> 01:47:08,307
A sequel, titled I Want to Believe, was released ten years later in 2008.

24
01:51:41,538 --> 01:51:47,538
The film explores themes of helicopter, space
marine, government, bomb, mutation.

25
01:56:20,769 --> 01:56:26,769
The tone is intense and thought_provoking and mind_bending.
